      <memberdef kind="function" id="classostk_1_1physics_1_1coordinate_1_1frame_1_1_manager_1a1cc9f57ac5406f7d25447ff2cb73958e" prot="public" static="no" const="no" explicit="no" inline="no" virt="non-virtual">
        <type>void</type>
        <definition>void ostk::physics::coordinate::frame::Manager::setMaxTransformCacheSize</definition>
        <argsstring>(const Size &amp;aMaxTransformCacheSize)</argsstring>
        <name>setMaxTransformCacheSize</name>
        <param>
          <type>const Size &amp;</type>
          <declname>aMaxTransformCacheSize</declname>
        </param>
        <briefdescription>
<para>Set the maximum number of cached transforms held per frame pair. </para>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para>The cache is keyed by frame pair and, within a pair, by instant. This bounds the number of instants held for each pair; once a pair reaches the bound, the instants cached for it are dropped and repopulated from scratch. Work that walks a long span of distinct instants — access generation, a propagation reported at fine cadence — will pass that bound, and past it very nearly every lookup misses. Sizing the cache to the number of distinct instants a run evaluates avoids that.</para>
<para>A larger cache is not free: it is trading memory for performance.</para>
<para>Lowering the bound drops any pair already holding more than the new bound.</para>
<para><programlisting><codeline><highlight class="normal">Manager::Get().setMaxTransformCacheSize(100000);</highlight></codeline>
</programlisting></para>
<para><parameterlist kind="param"><parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername direction="in">aMaxTransformCacheSize</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>A maximum transform cache size, strictly positive </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
</parameterlist>
</para>
        </detaileddescription>
        <inbodydescription>
        </inbodydescription>
